#slide()[
  #text(size: 30pt, weight: "bold")[Changing Fractions: Same Value, Different Look]
  #v(40pt)

  #only("1-")[- Pizza slices: 1/2 = 2/4 = 3/6 = 4/8 🍕]
  #v(20pt)
  #only("2-")[- Equivalent fractions: Same value, different appearance]
  #v(20pt)
  #only("3-")[- Key: Multiply numerator and denominator by same number]
  #v(20pt)
  #only("4-")[- Example: $1/2 = (1 dot 2)/(2 dot 2) = 2/4$]

  #only("1")[
    #voiceover("Imagine you're sharing a pizza with friends. Half a pizza can be cut into two slices, or four slices, or even six slices - it's still the same amount of pizza! This is exactly what we do when we change the denominator of a fraction.")
  ]
  #only("2")[
    #voiceover("These fractions that look different but represent the same amount are called equivalent fractions. They have the same value, just a different appearance.")
  ]
  #only("3")[
    #voiceover("The key to creating equivalent fractions is to multiply both the top number, called the numerator, and the bottom number, called the denominator, by the same number.")
  ]
  #only("4")[
    #voiceover("Let's look at an example. We can change one-half to two-fourths by multiplying both the numerator and denominator by two. One times two equals two, and two times two equals four. So one-half equals two-fourths.")
  ]
]


#slide()[
  #text(size: 30pt, weight: "bold")[The Magic Multiplication]
  #v(40pt)

  #only("1-")[- To change denominator: Multiply by form of 1]
  #v(20pt)
  #only("2-")[- Example: Change 3/4 to sixteenths]
  #v(20pt)
  #only("3-")[- Step 1: Find multiplier $(16/4 = 4)$]
  #v(20pt)
  #only("4-")[- Step 2: Multiply by $4/4 = 1$]
  #v(20pt)
  #only("5-")[- Result: $3/4 = (3 dot 4)/(4 dot 4) = 12/16$]

  #only("1")[
    #voiceover("To change the denominator of a fraction, we multiply it by a special form of the number one. This doesn't change the value of the fraction, but it does change how it looks.")
  ]
  #only("2")[
    #voiceover("Let's say we want to change three-fourths to an equivalent fraction with sixteenths in the denominator.")
  ]
  #only("3")[
    #voiceover("First, we need to find out what to multiply four by to get sixteen. Sixteen divided by four is four, so we'll multiply by four.")
  ]
  #only("4")[
    #voiceover("Now, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by four over four, which equals one. This doesn't change the value of the fraction.")
  ]
  #only("5")[
    #voiceover("So, three-fourths times four-fourths equals twelve-sixteenths. We've changed the denominator without changing the value of the fraction!")
  ]
]


#slide()[
  #text(size: 30pt, weight: "bold")[Recap: Changing Denominators]
  #v(40pt)

  #only("1-")[- Equivalent fractions: Same value, different look]
  #v(20pt)
  #only("2-")[- Method: Multiply numerator and denominator by same number]
  #v(20pt)
  #only("3-")[- Key idea: Multiply by a form of 1 (n/n = 1)]
  #v(20pt)
  #only("4-")[- Remember: Value stays the same, only appearance changes!]

  #only("1")[
    #voiceover("Let's recap what we've learned about changing the denominator of fractions.")
  ]
  #only("2")[
    #voiceover("We can create equivalent fractions by multiplying both the numerator and denominator by the same number.")
  ]
  #only("3")[
    #voiceover("The key idea is that we're really multiplying by a special form of the number one, like four-fourths or three-thirds.")
  ]
  #only("4")[
    #voiceover("Remember, when we change the denominator this way, the value of the fraction stays exactly the same - only its appearance changes. This is a powerful tool for working with fractions in many different situations!")
  ]
]